[configlet/util/base_test.py::do_test_add_rack](https://github.com/sonic-net/sonic-mgmt/blob/754c59af60a4481d81fb1b8b7fe936fc9db35809/tests/configlet/util/base_test.py#L268) is unconditionally checking for both IPv4 and IPv6 neighbor state in `tor_data`. If either is missing, the above error is seen. We need a mechanism that can be checked to see if we running on a topology that only expects one type of neighbor or the other. A [duthost_mgmt_ip()](https://github.com/sonic-net/sonic-mgmt/pull/20942/files#diff-375a0d59591c997eec44164d685e5990a6db2d7bc8c98dedb2e5c58d5804a311R853) fixture has been suggested that would at least tell us if we're in an IPv6-only network, which might work for the current use case. If there is ever a desire to have an IPv4-only network, I think we would have to account for that differently.

The other approach would be to try to read neighbor info from `tor_data` and if nothing is found for a given address family just skip it. If `tor_data` is considered a good enough source of truth, this would be a much easier solution.

@AntonHryshchuk @wangxin do we run any automated IPV6 testbed pipelines for GitHub PRs yet? I don't have access to the physical resources to set up an IPv6-only testbed, and the virtual testbed implementation we have set up doesn't allow for IPv6-only testbeds yet. I'm working on adding that functionality but it's taking me a longer than I would like, so if there is a way to open a PR and test changes against the community CI/CD infra I can take up adding the fix for this test issue.
